ent up over 300%  inmate on inmate assaults went up 46%  county jails were designed for short  term housing so when you take a  realigned population who are now former  felons with long sentences and you move  them to the county jail they're being  moved into facilities that were never  meant to hold them for years at a time  if you want to quantify it as a success  in ways that it lowered the state prison  population absolutely it's a success for  the state but I think in the grand  scheme of things if you want to measure  success I don't think we're ready to  measure it the governor I'm here to tell  you I don't believe it's wise or sound  public policy to release any more people  out of our prison other than in  accordance with our existing state law  and that is the reason why we are going  to court asking that the prison cap or  limit be removed in 2010 the California  prison guards Union spent more than two  million dollars supporting the election  of Governor Jerry Brown Governor Brown  of course has shown himself to be very  open to sentencing reform he was the one  who led the effort to pass realignment  but he's also made it very clear that  it's important not to jeopardize the  important changes already made and let  them sink in Jerry Brown has has been  kind of saying that since realignment  the only offenders left in prisons are  the most violent the most dangerous  criminals but that's not really true  actually there's a large number of drug  offenders and nonviolent property  offenders who are still in prison  because of the three strikes law the  second strike aspect of the law so  they've been sentenced as second  strikers ultimately if California wants  to get serious about prison reform  one-step it could take would be to  eliminate three strikes or at least only  have it applied to violent or serious  offenders drug policy is the major  driver in mass incarceration both in  California and nationwide incarceration  for other offenses is fairly st